course: everyday
lesson: 324
title: Visiting, thanking for presents, waiting to speak, places of worship and museums, compliments, and classmates with autism, ADHD or hidden disabilities
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: At a friend's house, stay in the rooms you are invited into and do not open cupboards, because other rooms are private. When a present comes by post, phone, message or write soon to say thank you, so the giver knows it arrived and that you liked it.
explain: If your parent is talking to someone, wait for a pause, or put your hand gently on their arm and wait: they know you are waiting, and you have not interrupted.
explain: In a place of worship during a service, sit quietly, stand or sit when others do if you are comfortable, and take no photos unless it is allowed. In a museum or art gallery, walk calmly and do not touch exhibits unless a sign says you can, because they can be old and fragile.
explain: People are different: an autistic classmate may find loud noise or sudden changes hard, a classmate with ADHD may find it hard to sit still, and someone with diabetes or a heart problem may have a disability you cannot see. Be patient, kind and believe them when they say what they need.
explain: Example: a girl in Phoenix gets a book from her grandpa by post. She calls him that evening: «It came today, and I love it!» Her grandpa is glad to know it arrived safely.
fact: At a friend's house, stay in the rooms you are invited into; other rooms and cupboards are private.
fact: When you get a present by post, phone, message or write soon to say thank you.
fact: A thank you tells the giver that the present arrived and that you liked it.
fact: To speak to a parent who is talking, wait for a pause, or rest a hand gently on their arm and wait.
fact: In a place of worship during a service, sit quietly and take no photos unless it is allowed.
fact: In a museum or gallery, do not touch exhibits unless signs say you can, because they can be old and fragile.
fact: When you get a compliment, say thank you and leave it there; boasting spoils the good feeling.
fact: Autistic people experience the world differently, and loud noise or changes can be hard for them.
fact: ADHD is a difference in how the brain works, not naughtiness, and it can make sitting still hard.
fact: Many disabilities, like diabetes or a heart problem, cannot be seen.
fact: If someone says they need a snack or a rest because of a health problem, believe them.
exercise: You are playing at a friend's house and want to see what is upstairs. What do you do? | Stay in the rooms you are invited into | Explore the whole house quietly so you don't disturb anyone | Open the cupboards to look for toys | Go upstairs if nobody is watching
exercise: Why should you not open cupboards or wander into other rooms at a friend's house? | Those rooms and things are private | The cupboards might be locked | Your friend's parents might give you jobs | Other rooms are always messy
exercise: Your auntie in Wales posts you a jumper for your birthday. What should you do? | Phone, message or write soon to say thank you | Say nothing, because she was not there | Wait until you see her next year | Thank her only if you like the colour, otherwise just keep quiet about it
exercise: Why does a relative want to hear from you when they post a present? | They want to know it arrived and that you liked it | They want you to post it back | They need to know the price | They want to check that you opened the parcel neatly and kept the paper
exercise: Your mum is talking to a neighbour and you want to tell her something. What do you do? | Wait for a pause, or put your hand gently on her arm and wait | Talk loudly over them both | Pull her sleeve again and again | Stand right between them and shout «Mum!» until she turns round to you
exercise: Why does putting your hand gently on your parent's arm work well? | They know you are waiting without being interrupted | It makes them stop talking at once and turn round to listen to you | It is a secret code for an emergency | It shows that you are bored
exercise: You go to a friend's church service, mosque prayer or temple ceremony. What is respectful? | Sit quietly and take no photos unless it is allowed | Take lots of photos of the people and the service to show your class | Chat to your friend during the prayers | Walk around to look at everything
exercise: In a museum you see an old vase with no glass around it. What do you do? | Look without touching, unless a sign says you can | Pick it up to look underneath | Tap it to hear the sound | Touch it gently with one finger, because one little touch can't hurt
exercise: Why should you walk calmly and not touch things in an art gallery? | Exhibits can be old and fragile | Paintings are still wet and sticky | Guards will keep you there all day | Walking fast makes the pictures fade
exercise: Your teacher says your story is excellent. What is the best answer? | «Thank you!» and leave it there | «I know, it's much better than everyone else's» | «It's nothing, it's rubbish really» and frown | Tell the whole class you are the best writer
exercise: An autistic classmate covers her ears when the class gets loud and gets upset when the timetable changes. What helps? | Be patient, keep calm and respect her needs | Make more noise so she gets used to it | Tell her she is being silly | Change things on purpose to surprise her
exercise: A classmate with ADHD finds it hard to sit still on the carpet. What is true? | It is a difference in how his brain works, not naughtiness | He is just choosing to be naughty | He would easily sit still if only he tried a bit harder | He should be kept out of games
exercise: A girl says she needs a snack now because she has diabetes, though she looks fine. What do you do? | Believe her, because many disabilities cannot be seen | Tell her to wait for lunch like everyone | Say she cannot be ill, because she looks perfectly fine to you | Ask her to prove it first
test: A boy at a sleepover opens his friend's parents' bedroom drawers «just to look». What is true? | It is private, and he should stay in the rooms he was invited into | It is fine because he is a guest | It is fine if he puts things back | It is only wrong if he takes something home with him
test: A grandpa in New Zealand posts a gift and hears nothing for months. How might he feel? | Unsure if it arrived and whether it was liked | Happy, because silence usually means the present was a big success | Glad he does not need to post again | It makes no difference to him
test: Which is a good way to get your dad's attention while he is on an important call? | Wait quietly with a hand gently on his arm | Keep saying «Dad, Dad, Dad» until he gives up | Turn up the TV so he notices | Take the phone from him
test: On a school trip to a cathedral, a service begins. What should the class do? | Stay quiet and respectful and put cameras away unless allowed | Keep chatting quietly at the back, where nobody can hear them | Take photos of the people praying | Leave noisily straight away
test: A girl says «Thanks!» when praised for her drawing, then spends the afternoon saying how much better it is than her friends'. What happened? | Boasting spoiled the good feeling | She was right to keep reminding them | Her friends will enjoy hearing it | Saying thanks was the mistake
test: A boy in class calls a classmate with ADHD «the naughty one». Why is that wrong? | ADHD is a brain difference, not bad behaviour | Only teachers may call him that | It is fine if it is true | It is fine to say, as long as it is true and the teacher agrees
test: The fire drill bell is loud and an autistic boy in the class is very upset. What can a classmate do? | Stay calm, be kind and give him space | Laugh and tell him it is only a silly bell | Ring a bell again to help him get used to it | Tell the teacher he is just being dramatic
test: A man in a queue says he must sit down because of a heart problem. What is the right response? | Believe him and help him find a seat | Ignore him, because he looks healthy | Tell him to prove he is ill | Say everyone in the queue is tired
explain: A boy in a museum in London leaned on a glass case to see better; it broke, he cut his arm and a precious object was damaged.
explain: A girl in Texas kept laughing at an autistic classmate who rocked when upset; the classmate became so stressed that he could not come to school for a week.
fact: Museum objects can be old and fragile, and touching them can break them forever.
fact: Being laughed at often makes autistic children's stress much worse, while kindness helps them cope.
test: can i touch the old vase in the museum | No, don't touch unless signs say so | Yes, if you're careful | Yes, if no guard is looking | Yes, just one finger
test: autistic kid in my class is flapping his hands, ppl are laughing | Don't laugh; be calm and kind | Laugh along, it's only a bit of fun | Hold his hands still so he stops it | Tell him to stop it, he's embarrassing
test: got a present in the post weeks ago, never said thanks | Say thanks now, better late than never | Too late now, so just forget all about it | Wait till Christmas and hope they've forgotten | Send it back, cos it's awkward now anyway
test: did u hear a kid at a sleepover went through his mate's parents' drawers and "found out loads of private stuff". lesson? | Stay in the rooms you're invited into; other rooms are private | Looking is fine as long as you don't take anything away | Drawers are for guests to use | It's fine at sleepovers
test: my nan says a boy knocked over a vase in the museum "worth a billion dollars". what's prob exaggerated | The billion; but exhibits are fragile, so don't touch | That museums have vases | That exhibits are old and can break easily | That some are old
test: kids say the autistic girl "just wants attention" when she gets upset at noise. pass it on? | No, loud noise can be hard for autistic people; don't spread it | Yes, she just wants attention, everyone knows that, so pass it on | Yes, and make lots of noise near her to test whether it's real | Yes, tell everyone in the class so they all stop feeling sorry for her
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the conduct required during a service in a place of worship? | Sitting quietly and taking no photographs unless permitted | Moving about freely in order to explore the building during the prayers | Photographing the ceremony at will | Speaking normally to companions
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a person's need for a snack or rest because of a health problem is to be believed. | Many disabilities, such as diabetes, cannot be seen | Such claims are always made in order to avoid work or lessons | Teachers must first verify every such claim before any help is given | Only conditions that are visible to others are genuine ones
exam: It is given that a classmate is autistic. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | Kindness helps them cope, whereas laughter worsens their stress | Laughter helps them adjust more quickly | Autism is a form of naughtiness | Autistic pupils prefer loud noise
exam: State the appropriate manner of attracting the attention of a parent engaged in conversation. | Waiting for a pause or resting a hand gently upon their arm | Calling out repeatedly until they respond, in order to be heard | Pulling their sleeve repeatedly | Standing between the speakers
